# No Refactoring Without Permission

## Do not refactor, reorganize, or rename existing code unless explicitly asked.

This includes:
- Renaming files, functions, or variables for "clarity"
- Moving files to "better" locations
- Restructuring folders
- Rewriting working code in a "cleaner" style
- Extracting code into new files when the current file is under the size limit
- Combining files that "belong together"

## When building a new feature:
- Add new code. Do not touch code that already works unless the feature requires it.
- If existing code needs a change to support the feature, change only the minimum needed.
- If you believe a refactor would help, say so and wait for approval. Do not do it.

## When explicitly asked to refactor:
- List every file that will change and what will change before starting
- Make the refactor in its own commit, separate from feature work
- Never refactor and add a feature in the same change

## Rewrites
Never rewrite a working section of the codebase from scratch. "Start over" on a module, page, or system is almost always worse than incremental improvement. If something feels like it needs a rewrite:
- Identify the specific problems (not "it's messy" — what exactly is broken or slow?)
- Propose targeted fixes for each specific problem
- Implement one fix at a time, with tests, committed separately
- A rewrite replaces working code with untested code. Targeted fixes preserve what works.
